<?nvs
// [ADR 0067](/docs/decisions/0067.md) § 4's one member that does not buffer,
// over the PostgreSQL `tests/db/compose.yaml` brings up. `Core\Db\Connection::stream`
// is `docs/spec/01-core-library.md` § 18's `Iterable<Db\Row>` row: the rows stay
// on the connection as a portal the server is still holding open, so walking ten
// thousand of them costs one row's memory rather than ten thousand.
//
// The connection it opens is named in root-owned config (§ 2), so `nvs.toml` owes
// this file both an `[[app]]` block granting `db.connect = ["main"]` and the
// `[db.main]` block behind the name; § 13's pool is what hands the connection back
// afterwards. The three lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal
// record under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

use Core\Db;
use Core\Db\Row;

var $db = Db::connect('main');

// Ten thousand rows the server generates, walked to the end. `stream` never
// answers a `Rows`, so there is no `count()` to read and the tally below is the
// walk's own evidence that it reached the last row.
var $stream = $db->stream('select generate_series(1, 10000) as n', []);

var $seen = 0;
var $peak = 0;
var $busy = 'allowed';

foreach ($stream as Row $row) {
    // What line 2 counts is what *this program* holds, and it says so rather
    // than overstating it: no `Core` member answers "how many rows is the
    // connection holding", so the fixture measures the half it can reach.
    // `$held` is declared inside the body, so it is the rows alive on this turn
    // of the loop and nothing more — a fixture that accumulated the walk would
    // declare it above the `foreach` and append, and would print 10000 here
    // while printing the same row count above.
    array<Row> $held = [$row];
    var $alive = Core\Arr::count($held) as int;
    if ($alive > $peak) {
        $peak = $alive;
    }

    var $n = $row->int('n');
    if ($n != null) {
        $seen = $seen + 1;
    }

    // The connection's half of the promise, and the reason line 3 rather than
    // line 2 is the evidence that nothing was buffered: a driver that had read
    // the result set into memory would have finished its statement, and this
    // `query` would have been answered instead of refused. It is asked on the
    // first row, with 9,999 still on the server, so the refusal is the portal
    // being open rather than a race at either end of the walk.
    //
    // § 4 makes the busy connection the program's mistake and not the network's,
    // so this is a `LogicError` and never an `IOError` — and the walk survives
    // it, which is the next 9,999 rows below.
    if ($seen == 1) {
        try {
            $db->query('select 1 as one', []);
        } catch (LogicError $refused) {
            $busy = 'LogicError';
        }
    }
}

echo 'streamed ', $seen, ' rows', "\n";
echo 'rows held at once: ', $peak, "\n";
echo 'a second statement on a streaming connection: ', $busy, "\n";
